# How can one move attack two things?
teaches: ["tactics/fork"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | calm, warm chess coach","b":"gemini:Puck | eager club player, relaxed and good-humoured; never exaggerated"}
prompt: "What makes a fork work: knight, queen and pawn forks? How do you set up a fork that is not there yet?"
next: {"title":"Why can't that piece move, and what is behind it?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/chess-30-days/09-why-can-t-that-piece-move-and-what-is.cast"}

## Warm-up: count, then calculate
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: 2r3k1/pb3ppp/1p6/3n4/8/2N5/PP3PPP/3RR1K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Nxd5, Bxd5, Rxd5]}
      - {fen: 3rr1k1/5ppp/1q6/8/8/8/4RPPP/4R1K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Rxe8+, Rxe8, Rxe8#]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    card title Warm-up

A: Today: how one move can attack two things at once. First, two warm-up puzzles from week one.
    draw position

B: Black's knight on d5 is attacked twice and defended once.
    highlight piece_d5

B: I take it with the rook.
    highlight piece_d1

A: Count it to the end. Rook first, and you swap a rook for a knight and a bishop: a pawn's worth.
    highlight piece_b7

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win the most material." widget chess line true hints ["Count every capture to the end.","Send the cheapest piece in first."] themes ["counting"] voice a teaches ["calculation/counting"]
        right: Knight first, rook last: you give a knight and win a knight and a bishop, a whole piece up.
        wrong: Count to the end of the trades: which piece should go in first?

B: So the cheapest piece goes in first, and the rook comes last.
    highlight piece_d5

A: Second one.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: Black is a whole queen up, and it's White to move.
    highlight piece_b6

B: Rook takes e8 is check. But then the other rook just takes back.
    highlight piece_d8

A: Don't stop there. After the recapture, it's your move again: checks, captures, threats.
    highlight piece_e1

    pause 0.3

    ask "Calculate to the end." widget chess line true hints ["Every move is a check or a capture.","Your second rook is waiting on e1."] themes ["back rank"] voice a teaches ["safety/back-rank"]
        right: Rook takes, rook takes back, rook takes again: mate on the back rank, and the queen never moves.
        wrong: After the recapture, look again: what can your second rook do?

B: Mate, a queen down. I'd have stopped after the first capture.
    point at.ref piece_g8 gesture circle

A: Counting and calculating are today's tools. A fork attacks two things at once, and only counting tells you whether it really wins.
    highlight piece_e8

## What makes a fork work
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r1q3k1/pp3ppp/8/3N1b2/8/8/PP2QPPP/3R2K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Nb6, axb6]}
      - {fen: r1q3k1/pp3ppp/8/3N1b2/8/8/PP2QPPP/3R2K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Ne7+, Kh8, Nxc8, Rxc8]}
      - {fen: 3q2k1/5ppp/8/8/4N3/8/5PPP/6K1 w - - 0 1, moves: []}
      - {fen: r5k1/p1q3pp/8/8/8/8/PP3PPP/3QR1K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Qd5+, Kf8, Qxa8+]}
      - {fen: r1bqkb1r/pppp1ppp/2n2n2/4p3/2B1P3/2N2N2/PPPP1PPP/R1BQK2R b KQkq - 5 4, moves: [Nxe4, Nxe4, d5, Bd3, dxe4, Bxe4], flip: true, arrows: [{from: d5, to: c4}, {from: d5, to: e4}]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0
    highlights:
      - e7
      - c8
      - g8
      - a8
sources:
    - id: fork
      title: Fork (chess)
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: Knights fork well because the pieces they attack cannot capture them and are worth more; a queen fork needs undefended targets or the king; the fork trick 4...Nxe4 5.Nxe4 d5 regains the piece.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fork_(chess)
    - id: lpdo
      title: Loose Pieces Drop Off (LPDO)
      authors: Karsten Müller (ChessBase)
      year: 2014
      finding: The acronym LPDO was made popular by John Nunn in Secrets of Practical Chess (1998).
      url: https://en.chessbase.com/post/loose-pieces-drop-off-lpdo

    card title "What makes a fork work"

A: With counting in hand, here's a fork. From d5, White's knight is one jump from attacking Black's queen and rook.
    draw position

B: Knight to b6! It attacks the queen and the rook at once.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

A: And the pawn on a7 just takes it.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.3

A: Rule one: a fork must land on a safe square.
    highlight piece_b6

A: There's a better fork here, on a square nobody guards. Find it.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Find the safe fork." widget chess line true hints ["A knight move with check.","Which square touches both the king and the queen?"] themes ["fork"] voice a
        right: Knight to e7, check: it hits the king, the queen, and the bishop on f5 too. The king steps aside, and the queen falls.
        wrong: Look for a knight move that gives check.

B: It's check, so Black has no time to save the queen.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 0.8

A: Rule two: a check makes a fork unanswerable. One move can't rescue the king and the queen.
    highlight piece_g8 piece_c8

A: Look at the colours. From a dark square, a knight only ever hits light squares.
    draw hl_e7 hl_c8 hl_g8 parallel true

B: So the king and the queen were both on light squares.
    point at.ref piece_c8 gesture circle

A: That's your scanner: two big pieces on one colour,
    highlight piece_g8 piece_c8

A: then look for the knight square that touches both.
    point at.ref sq_e7 gesture circle

    erase hl_e7 hl_c8 hl_g8

    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    quiz "King on g8, queen on d8: can a knight fork them?" voice a
        right: Never: g8 is a light square and d8 a dark one, and a knight's two targets always share a colour.
        wrong: Check each square's colour first.
        * Yes, from e7
        * Yes, from f7
        + Never: they stand on different colours

A: Queens fork too. White to move here.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: Queen to d5, check. And she hits the rook in the corner.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

A: Right, and it only works because that rook is loose: nothing guards it.
    draw hl_a8

A: A knight costs three, so it can fork anything bigger, guarded or not. A queen costs nine: she forks loose pieces, or the king.
    highlight piece_d5

B: Loose pieces drop off.
    animate plies_shown 3 duration 2

A: Exactly: the grandmaster John Nunn made that saying famous. Check your own loose pieces too.
    highlight piece_e1

    erase hl_a8

A: Last question: what if the fork isn't there yet?
    animate position_shown 4 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: A well-known opening shows how to make one. We're on Black's side of the board now.
    highlight piece_f6

B: Black just took a pawn with a knight.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

B: White takes the knight: a piece for a pawn.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.3

A: Now pawn to d5.
    animate plies_shown 3 duration 1.3

A: One pawn attacks the bishop and the knight.
    draw arrow_0 arrow_1 parallel true

A: It's called the fork trick: give something first, so the target lands on the fork square.
    highlight piece_e4

    erase arrow_0 arrow_1

A: Black gets the piece back, and material is level.
    animate plies_shown 6 duration 3

A: So a fork wants two targets, loose or bigger than the forker,
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 1 duration 0.6

A: a safe square, and best of all a check.
    highlight piece_e7

A: When one is missing, a capture can put it there.
    highlight piece_c8 piece_f5

## Your turn: six forks
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Six for six. Your knights have been hunting.
    good: Most of them. The rest will turn up in your games.
    poor: Forks hide well at first. Scan for loose pieces and try again.
    none: The puzzles will wait for you.
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r4rk1/pp3ppp/4b3/8/3n4/1BQ5/PP3PPP/5RK1 b - - 0 1, moves: [Ne2+, Kh1, Nxc3], flip: true}
      - {fen: r1b2rk1/pp2q1pp/8/n3p3/8/8/PPP2PPP/RNBQ1RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Qd5+, Be6, Qxa5]}
      - {fen: 2b3k1/ppp2ppp/3r1n2/8/4P3/1BN5/PP3PPP/4R1K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [e5, Rd2, exf6]}
      - {fen: r5k1/pp3ppp/1q6/8/8/8/PPnQ1PPP/R3R1K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Qxc2]}
      - {fen: 5rk1/pp4pp/8/2N1q3/8/8/PP4PP/5RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Rxf8+, Kxf8, Nd7+, Ke7, Nxe5]}
      - {fen: 2r2r1k/pp4pp/3pp3/8/4n3/1QN5/PP4PP/5RK1 b - - 0 1, moves: [Rxf1+, Kxf1, Nd2+, Ke2, Nxb3], flip: true}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    card title "Your turn"

A: Now you've seen what makes a fork work: your turn.
    draw position

A: Six puzzles, and the last two are forks you have to set up first.
    highlight piece_g8

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["A knight fork, with check.","The knight on d4."] themes ["fork"] voice a
        right: Knight to e2, check: the king steps aside, and the queen on c3 falls.
        wrong: Which knight move gives check and hits something big?

B: King and queen at once.
    highlight piece_c3

A: A royal fork, they call it. Next, the queen.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["Which black piece is loose?","A queen check."] themes ["fork"] voice a
        right: Queen to d5, check: once the check is blocked, the loose knight on a5 drops off.
        wrong: Find a black piece with no guard, then a queen check that also hits it.

B: The knight on a5 had no guard.
    highlight piece_a5

A: Next, the cheapest forker of all.
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win a piece." widget chess line true hints ["A pawn can fork too.","The pawn on e4."] themes ["fork"] voice a
        right: Pawn to e5 hits the rook and the knight: the rook runs, and the knight falls to a pawn.
        wrong: Which pawn move attacks two black pieces at once?

A: Now Black's knight has forked your rooks.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: I'd save the rook on a1... or the other one?
    highlight piece_a1 piece_e1

    pause 0.3

    ask "What do you play?" widget chess line true hints ["The Three Questions: any captures?","Is that knight guarded?"] themes ["hanging piece"] voice a teaches ["safety/hanging-pieces"]
        right: Take the knight: a forking piece that stands loose is just a free piece.
        wrong: Before you save a rook, look at the knight itself.

A: Now the fork that isn't there yet. First, bring the king to the square.
    animate position_shown 4 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Set up a fork." widget chess line true hints ["A capture with check.","Where will the king stand after it takes back?"] themes ["fork","decoy"] voice a teaches ["tactics/fork","tactics/decoy"]
        right: Rook takes f8, check: the king must take back, and knight to d7 forks the king and the queen.
        wrong: Trade first, with check. Then look where the king ends up.

A: The same trick, from Black's side.
    animate position_shown 5 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Set up a fork." widget chess line true hints ["Trade on f1, with check.","Then the knight on e4."] themes ["fork","decoy"] voice a teaches ["tactics/fork","tactics/decoy"]
        right: Rook takes f1, check: the king takes back, and knight to d2 forks the king and the queen on b3.
        wrong: Which capture brings White's king onto a square your knight can reach?

A: So before every move, scan for two targets, loose or big, within one move's reach.
    highlight piece_b3

A: If they aren't lined up yet, a capture with check can line them up: that's how this king got dragged out.
    highlight piece_e2

A: Homework: one slow game today, fifteen plus ten or slower. Then find its first mistake.
    erase position

## Where next
end_page: true

    text end_next_kicker "Next" x 500 y 540 font_size 26 style.opacity 0.6
